---
/**
 * Where the record comes from, what is included, and what the site does
 * with votes that have no per-member roll.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout
  title="About — VotedFor.Us"
  description="Where votedfor.us gets its data, which votes appear, and how unanimous consent and voice votes are shown."
>
  <section class="section stack about">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">The votes, as the record has them.</p>

    <p>
      VotedFor.Us is an unofficial record of floor votes in the 119th Congress.
      It is not a government site and has no official standing. The source is
      <a href="https://www.congress.gov">Congress.gov</a>. This site displays
      that record. It does not invent, filter, or rewrite who voted.
    </p>

    <h2>Which bills appear</h2>
    <p>
      A bill is on this site when it has at least one recorded vote. Bills that
      have only been referred to committee, and bills with no floor vote we
      count, are not listed. “Recorded” here means one of three chamber actions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Roll call.</strong> Yeas and nays were taken. The roll names
        each member and the cast Congress published.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unanimous consent.</strong> The chamber agreed without a roll
        call. Congress publishes the outcome, not a per-member map.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Voice vote.</strong> The chamber acted by voice. Counted only
        when the action does not say someone demanded the yeas and nays — that
        demand is a later roll call, and that roll is the record.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Consent is shown as Yea</h2>
    <p>
      Any member may demand a recorded vote. Not demanding one is consent to
      the outcome. For unanimous consent and voice votes the site still names
      who was serving in that chamber that day, and the card reads as a Yea.
      The vote kind is always named beside that position. A bare “voted Yea”
      without the kind would overstate the record.
    </p>
    <p>
      The underlying data keeps those votes’ member maps empty and lists
      membership separately. The Yea is a display inference, stated here, not
      a cast Congress published.
    </p>

    <h2>Passed, rejected, and bill state</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Passed</strong> — the official result indicates agreement (passed, agreed to, confirmed, or passed by unanimous consent).</li>
      <li><strong>Rejected</strong> — failed, rejected, or a voice vote where the noes prevailed.</li>
      <li><strong>Became law</strong> — signed or became public law.</li>
      <li><strong>Failed</strong> — failed of passage, vetoed, or a rejected motion that ends the path.</li>
      <li><strong>In progress</strong> — anything else.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What changes</h2>
    <p>
      Data is regenerated from Congress.gov. Each update is listed on the
      <a href={`${base}changelog`}>changelog</a>.
    </p>

    <nav class="form-actions">
      <a href={base}>Find a member or a bill</a>
      <a href={`${base}bills/119`}>All bills of the 119th</a>
      <a href={`${base}members`}>All members</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about .lede {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .about p,
  .about li {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .about ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.2em;
  }
</style>
